course: dictionary
lesson: 8967
title: Sinew: a tough cord in the body
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: You do not need to know every word to understand a text. When you meet a new word, read the words around it, because they leave clues. Today's word is sinew.
explain: «His mendy-bag holds reindeer-sinews (tough strings from an animal's body), strips of leather, bee's-wax and resin (sticky tree-gum).» The brackets explain the word for you: a sinew is a tough string from a body, not a bag or a bird. The list of things beside it also shows it is something you could use to make things.
explain: In your own body, a sinew (also called a tendon) is a tough cord that joins a muscle to a bone. When a muscle pulls, the sinew pulls the bone, so you can move. The Achilles tendon, at the back of your heel, is a famous example.
explain: Sinews can also mean strength or power, as in «strain every sinew», which means try as hard as you possibly can. To check, swap in your meaning: «try as hard as you can» still makes sense, so you have understood it.
fact: A sinew is a tendon: a tough cord in the body that joins a muscle to a bone.
fact: The Achilles tendon is the sinew at the back of the heel.
fact: «Strain every sinew» means try as hard as you possibly can.
fact: «The sinews of war» means the money and supplies needed for war.
fact: Long ago, people used animal sinew as thread and for bowstrings.
exercise: «His mendy-bag holds reindeer-sinews (tough strings from an animal's body), strips of leather, bee's-wax and resin.» What are reindeer-sinews? | A kind of small wooden box | Sweet, sticky tree-gum | Strips of leather cut for shoes | *Tough strings from an animal's body
exercise: What does a sinew join together in the body? | Two muscles on the same arm | A heart and a lung | *A muscle and a bone | Two bones
exercise: Ayesha reads «strain every sinew». What does it mean? | Sit still and stay quiet | Stretch out slowly and gently | *Try as hard as you possibly can | Lose your voice from shouting
test: In «the sinews of war», what do sinews mean? | Tough strings from a bow | Bones in the arm | Long, thin bags | *Money and supplies needed for war
test: Which part of the body is the Achilles tendon? | A bone in the knee | *The sinew at the back of the heel | A soft muscle in the stomach | The strong bit of the wrist
exam: Jayden meets the word «sinew» in «muscle and sinew». What is the best way to work out its meaning? | *Read the words around it and swap in a meaning to check | Skip the word and never think about it again | Look only at the first letter | Guess that it means a bag
